BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:instituto-sacatar-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Instituto Sacatar: Sacatar Fellowship 2027 (Bahia\, Brazi
 l)
DESCRIPTION:Brazil's longest-running artist residency\, on the island of It
 aparica across the bay from Salvador\, hosting six to eight artists per se
 ssion for roughly eight weeks. Open to all creative disciplines and\, unus
 ually for this region\, to all nationalities\, which makes it one of the f
 ew genuinely international residencies in Latin America. Sacatar provides 
 full hosting (private room\, three meals daily\, individual studio\, laund
 ry\, local logistics and connections) but explicitly does NOT provide a st
 ipend\, airfare to Salvador\, art materials or discretionary local expense
 s\, so applicants must fund their own travel. Individual artists only\; gr
 oup proposals are not accepted and former Sacatar Fellows may not reapply.
  COVID vaccination is required. NOTE: there is a USD 25 application fee. A
 pply through SlideRoom by 28 August 2026. Contact info@sacatar.org.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Instituto Sacatar\nLocation: Itaparica island\, Bahia\, Brazil.
  Open to all nationalities.\nAward: Fully hosted residency: private bedroo
 m with attached bathroom\, three meals a day (except Saturday evenings\, S
 undays and Brazilian holidays)\, an individual studio suited to the discip
 line\, weekly laundry\, logistical support and airport transfer from Salva
 dor. NO STIPEND\, no airfare and no materials budget. USD 25 application f
 ee. One French artist may additionally receive airfare and a stipend throu
 gh Villa Fatumbi.\nDuration: Approximately eight weeks\; specific session 
 dates are listed in the application.\n\nMore: https://sacatar.org/en/fello
 wships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sacatar.org/en/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Itaparica island\, Bahia\, Brazil. Open to all nationalities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kometa-fellows-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kometa Fellows 2026: Field Journalism\, Photography\, and
  Writing
DESCRIPTION:Kometa\, a French literary and journalism magazine\, awards fel
 lowships to journalists\, writers\, and photographers documenting reality 
 in contexts of war\, censorship\, or severe social and political tensions.
  Projects must be rooted in direct field experience and focused on contemp
 orary rather than historical events. Projects already under a contractual 
 agreement are ineligible. AMOUNT: €10\,000 per Fellow\, six Fellows sele
 cted. WHAT FELLOWS RECEIVE: one year of funding\; publication of the compl
 eted project (partial or full) in Kometa\; opportunity to contribute to th
 e monthly newsletter\; mentorship through editorial collaboration. 2026 FO
 CUS AREAS: disinformation and narrative wars\; political and social change
 s in the Gulf. DEADLINE: 31 July 2026. APPLY via the Tally form (https://t
 ally.so/r/J9XxMK)\, in French or English (about 30 to 45 minutes). Contact
 : fellows@kometarevue.com.\n\nOrganisation: Kometa (French literary and jo
 urnalism magazine)\nLocation: Remote / field-based (international)\nAward:
  €10\,000 per Fellow\, six Fellows selected. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: One year of funding for field work and research\n\nMore: https://komet
 arevue.com/page/fellows?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://kometarevue.com/page/fellows?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / field-based (international)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mountainfilm-commitment-grants-emerging-filmmaker-fellowship-2026@artif
 icialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260717
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mountainfilm: Commitment Grants & Emerging Filmmaker Fell
 owship
DESCRIPTION:Mountainfilm's Commitment Grants support documentary filmmakers
  with projects in post-production\, with over USD 90\,000 in funding this 
 cycle for films that celebrate adventure\, activism\, social justice\, cul
 ture\, the environment\, and indomitable spirit. Mountainfilm actively enc
 ourages diversity behind the camera\, and a portion of funding is set asid
 e for two focus areas: projects featuring an LGBTQ+ protagonist or a story
  about overcoming personal challenges\, societal stigmas\, and/or communit
 y obstacles with a meaningful connection to the outdoors or nature-based p
 ursuits\; and projects exploring immigration or migration\, or created by 
 immigrant or first-generation immigrant filmmakers. AWARDS: individual Com
 mitment Grants range from USD 3\,000 to 6\,000. EMERGING FILMMAKER FELLOWS
 HIP: one early-career filmmaker is selected from the Commitment Grant appl
 icant pool for a yearlong mentorship that includes edit and story consulta
 tion\, networking opportunities\, a cash award\, and a funded trip to Tell
 uride during the festival over Memorial Day weekend (travel\, lodging\, an
 d a Mountainfilm pass\, applicable to the year following the award). ELIGI
 BILITY: Commitment Grants are open to applicants from any country\; the Em
 erging Filmmaker Fellowship accepts applicants from any country but requir
 es current U.S. residency. Post-production projects only\, completed\, rel
 eased\, or festival-screened projects are not eligible\, and impact campai
 gns are not funded. Prior Commitment Grant recipients should not re-apply 
 for the same project\; previously declined projects may re-apply only with
  substantial changes. REQUIREMENTS: a video sample or trailer of the curre
 nt project no longer than 10 minutes (password-protected Vimeo links prefe
 rred)\; the screener link must stay active and cannot be changed after sub
 mission. The Emerging Filmmaker application additionally requires a direct
 or bio\, headshot\, and cover letter. Recipients are expected to provide p
 rogress updates 2-3 times a year\, include Mountainfilm recognition in cre
 dits\, and discuss premiere consideration with the programming team. TIMEL
 INE: applications are open July 1 to July 16\, 2026 (closing 11:59 PM MDT)
 \, with notifications in late August or early September. Questions: entrie
 s@mountainfilm.org.\n\nOrganisation: Mountainfilm\nLocation: Application o
 nline\; Telluride\, Colorado\, United States (fellowship trip during the f
 estival). Commitment Grants are open to filmmakers from any country\; the 
 Emerging Filmmaker Fellowship requires current U.S. residency.\nAward: Ove
 r USD 90\,000 in total funding. Individual Commitment Grants range from US
 D 3\,000 to 6\,000. The Emerging Filmmaker Fellowship adds a yearlong ment
 orship\, a cash award\, and a funded trip to Telluride over Memorial Day w
 eekend (travel\, lodging\, and a festival pass).\nDuration: Commitment Gra
 nts support projects in post-production\; the Emerging Filmmaker Fellowshi
 p is a yearlong mentorship.\n\nMore: https://www.mountainfilm.org/mountain
 film-initiatives/commitment-grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mountainfilm.org/mountainfilm-initiatives/commitment-grants
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Application online\; Telluride\, Colorado\, United States (fellows
 hip trip during the festival). Commitment Grants are open to filmmakers fr
 om any country\; the Emerging Filmmaker Fellowship requires current U.S. r
 esidency.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:this-is-gender-regional-curatorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260922
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260923
SUMMARY:Deadline: This is Gender Regional Curatorial Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:This is Gender\, the photography and visual culture platform ru
 n by Global 50/50\, is launching its inaugural Regional Curatorial Fellows
  Programme for early-career curators and cultural practitioners working ac
 ross photography\, visual culture and gender justice. The premise: images 
 shape how gender\, power and social realities are understood\, feeding pub
 lic debate\, cultural memory\, research\, advocacy and policy\, yet the co
 mmunities and visual practices that should inform those narratives stay un
 derrepresented in the institutions that circulate them. The programme's ar
 gument is that diversifying who makes images is not enough\, and that it a
 lso matters who selects\, contextualises\, writes about\, commissions and 
 circulates them. For the first time\, This is Gender is embedding regional
  curators directly inside its global photography competition\, which has r
 un six cycles and receives thousands of submissions a year. WHAT THE FELLO
 WSHIP INVOLVES: working inside the competition and seeing first-hand how a
  major platform discovers\, reviews and champions new work\; shaping curat
 orial decisions by bringing regional knowledge and cultural insight into t
 he selection and interpretation of work\; contributing to public-facing pr
 ojects including exhibitions\, editorial outputs and digital storytelling 
 while developing your own writing\, framing and curatorial voice\; direct 
 engagement with international judges\, curators\, artists\, editors and cu
 ltural practitioners\; and building a network across an international coho
 rt of peers. WHO SHOULD APPLY: curators\, critics\, writers\, researchers\
 , artistic directors\, photographers\, organisers and cultural practitione
 rs from global majority countries and historically underrepresented commun
 ities. Early-career practitioners and recent graduates are strongly encour
 aged\, and a formal curatorial title is not required. HOW TO APPLY: read t
 he full role description and FAQs on the fellowship page and submit the on
 line application form\; the organisers offer the application in alternativ
 e formats and are open to a conversation before applying. Applications clo
 se 22 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: This is Gender (Global 50/50)\nLoca
 tion: Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competiti
 on. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority countri
 es and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Africa
 \, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities w
 orldwide.\nAward: Stipend of up to GBP 3\,000 for the seven-month part-tim
 e fellowship. No application fee.\nDuration: Seven months from October 202
 6\, part-time and flexible\n\nMore: https://thisisgender.global5050.org/re
 gional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://thisisgender.global5050.org/regional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competi
 tion. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority count
 ries and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Afri
 ca\, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities
  worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bow-seat-true-blue-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: True Blue Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bow Seat's True Blue Fellowship funds youth-led projects that u
 se the creative arts as their primary method for building awareness or dri
 ving action on ocean\, waterways and climate change issues\, including env
 ironmental justice. Twelve fellows are selected each year and receive up t
 o USD 2\,500 in project funding alongside mentorship\, capacity-building p
 rogramming\, and access to a peer community that includes the Future Blue 
 Youth Council and Bow Seat staff. ELIGIBILITY: youth worldwide aged 16 to 
 24 at the start of the fellowship period in January. Individuals\, groups 
 and youth-led organisations may apply\, with one designated project leader
  for a group. Projects must be new or emerging\, meaning less than two yea
 rs old\, and should span at least one year running January to December. Ap
 plications must be submitted in English\, though translation tools are acc
 eptable\, and applicants should be comfortable with spoken English for pee
 r collaboration. NOTE: this is a youth programme with an upper age limit o
 f 24\, and the creative-arts requirement is central rather than incidental
 \, so proposals built on other methods are out of scope. Deadline 30 Augus
 t 2026 at 11:59pm ET.\n\nOrganisation: Bow Seat Ocean Awareness Programs\n
 Location: Remote\, open to youth worldwide.\nAward: Up to USD 2\,500 in pr
 oject funding per fellow\, plus mentorship and capacity-building programmi
 ng. Twelve youth are selected annually. No application fee.\nDuration: One
  year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://bowseat.org/programs/tru
 e-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://bowseat.org/programs/true-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to youth worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adina-talve-goodman-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T205603Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261013
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261014
SUMMARY:Deadline: One Story: Adina Talve-Goodman Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:Annual educational fellowship honouring the memory of author an
 d former One Story managing editor Adina Talve-Goodman: a year-long mentor
 ship on the craft of fiction writing with One Story magazine\, aimed at gi
 ving a writer outside the fold a significant career boost. WHAT THE FELLOW
  RECEIVES: access to One Story online classes\; admission to the Writing C
 ircle\; a USD 2\,000 stipend and free admission to the week-long summer wr
 iters' conference with craft lectures\, an intensive fiction workshop and 
 panels with literary agents and publishers\; and a full manuscript review 
 and consultation with executive editor Hannah Tinti (story collection or n
 ovel in progress up to 150 pages / 35\,000 words). ELIGIBILITY: early-care
 er fiction writers who have not yet published a book\, have never been enr
 olled in an advanced degree programme (MA or MFA) in creative writing\, En
 glish or literature and have no plans to attend one in 2027\, and are at l
 east 21 years old as of 1 January 2027. Applicants outside the United Stat
 es are welcome. The fellowship seeks writing that centres\, celebrates or 
 reclaims being marginalised through the lens of race\, ethnicity\, gender\
 , sexuality\, age\, class\, religion\, illness\, disability\, trauma\, mig
 ration\, displacement\, dispossession or imprisonment. APPLY via Submittab
 le with a fiction sample of 3\,000 to 5\,000 words (multiple stories allow
 ed within the limit\, one file)\, a personal statement of 400 to 750 words
 \, a statement of purpose of 200 to 350 words\, two references (contact de
 tails only\, no letters) and a current resume including writing classes\, 
 awards\, publications and residencies. Applications open 8 September and c
 lose 13 October 2026 at 23:59 ET\; the winner is announced in January 2027
 .\n\nOrganisation: One Story (with the Talve-Goodman Family)\nLocation: Re
 mote year-long mentorship with One Story magazine (Brooklyn\, US)\; includ
 es free admission to the week-long summer writers' conference. Open to app
 licants outside the United States.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend plus free ad
 mission to One Story's week-long summer writers' conference\, access to On
 e Story online classes\, admission to the Writing Circle\, and a full manu
 script review and consultation with Executive Editor Hannah Tinti (story c
 ollection or novel in progress up to 150 pages / 35\,000 words). No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: Year-long fellowship for 2027. Applications open 8 S
 eptember 2026 and close 13 October 2026 at 23:59 ET\; winner announced pub
 licly in January 2027.\n\nMore: https://one-story.com/learn/fellowship/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://one-story.com/learn/fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote year-long mentorship with One Story magazine (Brooklyn\, US
 )\; includes free admission to the week-long summer writers' conference. O
 pen to applicants outside the United States.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
